# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
QUIZ = {"03": [
    ("Who directs the response during an event in a dialysis unit?",
     ["The charge nurse, with the physician-on-duty", "The safety nurse", "The newest technician", "The unit owner by phone"], 0,
     "Best: the people with clinical responsibility for the patients on the shift command. Why not the others: the safety nurse plans and drills but does not command; the owner and a new technician do not direct care."),
    ("What is the safety nurse&rsquo;s role during a real event?",
     ["Supports the incident lead; does not command", "Takes command of the whole unit", "Leaves to update the plan", "Decides on early dialysis"], 0,
     "Best: the safety nurse plans, drills and helps, but command stays with the charge nurse and physician. Why not the others: early dialysis is the nephrologist&rsquo;s decision."),
    ("What is the lead time for PAGASA Tropical Cyclone Wind Signal No. 2?",
     ["Winds of 62 to 88 km/h within 24 hours", "Winds of 39 to 61 km/h within 36 hours", "Winds of 118 to 184 km/h within 12 hours", "Winds of 89 to 117 km/h within 18 hours"], 0,
     "Best: Signal No. 2 means gale-force winds within 24 hours. Why not the others: those are Signals No. 1, 4 and 3."),
    ("Why does each unit set its own actions for each signal level?",
     ["Because hazards differ: a unit that floods at Signal No. 2 cannot wait for No. 4", "Because PAGASA requires it", "Because signals are not reliable", "Because the DOH sets one action list for all"], 0,
     "Best: the trigger is national; the action depends on the unit&rsquo;s own risks. Why not the others: none is true."),
    ("Which communication channel often still works when voice calls fail?",
     ["Text messages", "Fax", "Email only", "Landline only"], 0,
     "Best: text messages often get through on overloaded networks; build several channels. Why not the others: they are less reliable or not always available."),
    ("In what order does a typical call-down run?",
     ["Staff first, then patients due in the next 24 hours, then the rest", "Patients alphabetically", "The unit owner, then everyone else", "Patients first, staff last"], 0,
     "Best: knowing who can come in decides what the unit can offer. Why not the others: alphabetical or owner-first ignores risk and capacity."),
    ("What must a receiving-unit agreement name?",
     ["A contact person and deputy, capacity, what you will send, and transport", "Only the other unit&rsquo;s address", "The price per session only", "Nothing; a phone call on the day is enough"], 0,
     "Best: an agreement works only if someone answers and knows what to expect. Why not the others: they leave out what makes the transfer safe."),
    ("A freestanding unit coordinates evacuation routes and warnings with which office?",
     ["The local disaster risk reduction and management office (LDRRMO)", "PhilHealth", "The Bureau of Customs", "The Professional Regulation Commission"], 0,
     "Best: Republic Act No. 10121 set up local disaster offices for this. Why not the others: they do not coordinate local disaster response."),
    ("Where should the one-page plan be kept?",
     ["Posted beside the nurses&rsquo; station and in the grab bag", "Locked in the manager&rsquo;s office", "Only on the unit&rsquo;s computer", "With the machine manuals"], 0,
     "Best: anyone on the shift must be able to open it, even without power. Why not the others: they make it hard to reach in an event."),
    ("The incident lead cannot be reached when Signal No. 2 is raised. What happens?",
     ["The named deputy activates the plan and informs the manager and clinical head", "Nothing until the lead answers", "The patients decide", "The plan is cancelled"], 0,
     "Best: every role has a deputy so the plan does not wait. Why not the others: waiting loses the lead time."),
]}
